# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
E=[]
def e(id,title,kw,a,act="",rel="",pri=0):
    E.append(dict(id=id,title=title,kw=[k.strip() for k in kw.split(",") if k.strip()],a=a,act=act,rel=[r for r in rel.split(",") if r],pri=pri))

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- CRISIS (priority)
e("suicide","I want to end my life","suicide,suicidal,kill myself,end my life,want to die,wanna die,better off dead,no reason to live,take my own life,end it all,dont want to be here,can't go on,cant go on,not worth living",
"I'm really glad you told me. You matter, and you don't have to carry this alone.\n\nPlease reach out to someone right now:\n• SADAG Suicide Crisis Line: 0800 567 567 (24 hours)\n• Lifeline: 0861 322 322\n• In immediate danger: 112\n\nIf you can, tell someone near you how you feel and stay with them. Put anything you could hurt yourself with out of reach. These feelings can change, and help works.","mind","selfharm,depression,therapy",1)
e("selfharm","Self-harm urges","self harm,self-harm,hurt myself,cutting myself,cut myself,harming myself,urge to hurt",
"Thank you for being honest. The urge to hurt yourself is a sign of deep pain, not a weakness, and you deserve care instead of more pain.\n\n• Reach out now: SADAG 0800 21 22 23 or the Suicide Crisis Line 0800 567 567.\n• Tell a person you trust how you feel, even just 'I'm struggling'.\n• Move to a space where others are around, and keep sharp objects away.\n• If you have hurt yourself badly, go to a clinic or call 112.\n\nA counsellor can help you find safer ways to cope.","mind","suicide,anxiety,therapy",1)
e("rape","I was raped or sexually assaulted","raped,rape,sexually assaulted,sexual assault,molested,forced me to have sex,forced himself,sexual violence,assaulted me sexually,touched me without,drugged and,i was abused sexually",
"I'm so sorry. It is not your fault, and you deserve care and support.\n\n1. Get to a safe place and, if you can, call someone you trust.\n2. Go to a hospital, clinic or Thuthuzela Care Centre. HIV PEP works best within 72 hours, and emergency contraception works best as early as possible.\n3. If you can, avoid washing or changing clothes before an exam, as this may preserve evidence. If you already have, you can still get care and report.\n4. A doctor completes a medical report (the J88 form) if you decide to report.\n5. GBV Command Centre (free, 24/7): 0800 428 428.\n\nYou choose if and when to report. Counselling is available whatever you decide.","info","afterassault,reporting,gbvhelp,trauma",1)
e("danger","I feel unsafe right now","following me,followed,attacked,in danger,unsafe right now,kidnap,someone is chasing,threatening me,he is outside,stalker outside,scared for my life,help me now,being chased,someone is following",
"Your safety comes first.\n\n• Head to a busy, well-lit place (shop, petrol station, security guard).\n• Call 112 or 10111 now.\n• Tap SOS in HerHub to alert your trusted circle with your location.\n• If you are being followed, don't go home. Go to people.\n• Make noise if needed: shout, or use the siren in HerHub.","sos","walkhome,drivesafe,selfdefence,sirenhelp",1)
e("abuse_now","My partner is abusive","abusive partner,hits me,beats me,hit me,domestic violence,violent partner,boyfriend hit,husband hit,he beats me,he hits me,my partner hurts,afraid of my husband,afraid of my boyfriend,choked me,strangled",
"Thank you for trusting me. What's happening isn't your fault, and help is available.\n\n• GBV Command Centre (free, 24/7): 0800 428 428\n• Police: 10111, or 112 in an emergency\n• You can apply for a protection order at the magistrate's court for free\n• Strangulation is very dangerous even when there are no marks. Seek medical care.\n• Build a safety plan in HerHub, and use Quick Exit if you need to hide the app\n\nWould you like to know how to leave safely, or how a protection order works?","abuse","leave_abuser,protection_order,safety_plan,signs_abuse",1)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- GBV and legal
e("signs_abuse","Signs of an abusive relationship","signs of abuse,red flags,is it abuse,is my relationship abusive,toxic relationship,emotional abuse,verbal abuse,psychological abuse,gaslighting,belittles me,calls me names,isolates me,jealous and controlling",
"Abuse is not only hitting. Common warning signs:\n• Constant criticism, name-calling or humiliation\n• Controlling who you see, where you go, your phone or your money\n• Jealousy and accusations, checking your messages\n• Threats, scaring you, breaking things\n• Making you feel guilty or 'crazy' (gaslighting)\n• Pressuring you into sex\n• Cycles of cruelty followed by apologies and gifts\n\nIf several of these sound familiar, your feelings are valid. The GBV Command Centre (0800 428 428) can talk it through confidentially.","abuse","coercive,leave_abuser,safety_plan,protection_order")
e("coercive","Coercive control","coercive control,controlling behaviour,controlling partner,controls my money,wont let me work,tracks my phone,checks my phone,monitors me,restricts me",
"Coercive control is a pattern of behaviour that takes away your freedom: controlling money, movement, clothes, friends, work, or monitoring your phone. It can happen without any physical violence and is still abuse.\n\nWhat helps:\n• Talk to someone outside the relationship\n• Keep a private record of incidents (HerHub's private log)\n• Make a safety plan before any big change\n• Call 0800 428 428 for free counselling and options","abuse","signs_abuse,economic_abuse,safety_plan")
e("economic_abuse","Financial or economic abuse","economic abuse,financial abuse,controls my money,takes my salary,takes my card,won't let me have money,hides money,forces me to sign,my partner spends my money,debt in my name",
"Financial abuse includes taking your salary or grants, hiding household money, forbidding you to work, running up debt in your name, or forcing you to sign documents.\n\n• Open an account in your name only, if you can do so safely\n• Keep copies of your ID, payslips and bank statements somewhere safe\n• You may apply for maintenance for yourself and your children at the magistrate's court\n• Legal Aid South Africa is free: 0800 110 110\n• Check your credit report for debts you didn't take","finance","maintenance,coercive,legalaid")
e("protection_order","How to get a protection order","protection order,restraining order,interdict,domestic violence act,apply for protection,peace order,get an order against,court order against him",
"In South Africa you can apply for a protection order under the Domestic Violence Act, free of charge.\n\n1. Go to the magistrate's court nearest you (or the police can help you).\n2. Ask for the domestic violence clerk and complete the application. They must assist you.\n3. A magistrate may grant an interim order straight away. It is served on the abuser, with a date to return to court.\n4. On the return date the court decides on a final order.\n5. Once he has been served, breaking the order is a crime. Keep a copy with you and note any breach.\n\nYou don't need a lawyer. Legal Aid: 0800 110 110. This is general information, not legal advice.","abuse","leave_abuser,reporting,harassment_order,legalaid")
e("harassment_order","Protection from stalking or harassment","harassment act,protection from harassment,stalking order,harassment order,online harassment order,stalker order,keeps messaging me,wont leave me alone",
"The Protection from Harassment Act lets you apply for an order against someone who stalks or harasses you, including online, even if you were never in a relationship.\n\n• Apply at the magistrate's court (harassment court clerk)\n• Keep screenshots, call logs and dates as evidence\n• Block, but save what they sent first\n• You can also report to the police and ask for a case number\n\nLegal Aid can help for free: 0800 110 110.","abuse","stalking,cyberstalk,reporting")
e("leave_abuser","Leaving an abusive relationship safely","how to leave,leave my partner,leave him,leaving an abusive,escape abuse,get out of the relationship,run away from home,safe house,where can i go,need to leave tonight",
"Leaving is the most dangerous time, so plan if you can.\n\n• Call the GBV Command Centre (0800 428 428). They can help find a shelter or safe house.\n• Pack a hidden bag: ID, bank card, phone charger, medication, spare keys, some cash, children's documents.\n• Choose a safe person who knows your plan and a code word to signal danger.\n• Leave when the abuser is out, if possible, and don't tell him where you're going.\n• Apply for a protection order and maintenance afterwards.\n• If you are in danger now, call 112 or 10111.\n\nYour private safety plan in HerHub can store your checklist.","abuse","safety_plan,protection_order,shelter,children_abuse")
e("safety_plan","Make a safety plan","safety plan,plan to be safe,emergency bag,escape plan,exit plan,go bag",
"A safety plan is your personal checklist for staying safe. Include:\n• Warning signs and safe rooms in the home (with exits, avoid kitchen or bathroom)\n• A hidden emergency bag and spare keys\n• Trusted people and a code word\n• Important numbers: 112, 10111, 0800 428 428\n• Copies of ID, protection order and medical records\n• Where you would go and how you would get there\n\nOpen HerHub's Abuse & Harassment Support to build and keep yours privately.","abuse","leave_abuser,shelter")
e("shelter","Shelters and safe houses","shelter,safe house,place to stay,somewhere to sleep,women's shelter,refuge,nowhere to go,homeless,safe place to stay",
"Shelters give women (and often children) a safe place to stay, plus counselling and practical help.\n\n• Call the GBV Command Centre: 0800 428 428. They can refer you to a shelter near you.\n• The Department of Social Development and local police stations also keep lists of shelters.\n• Ask whether the shelter takes children and what to bring.\n• Many shelters keep their address private for your safety.\n\nIf you are in danger tonight, call 112 or 10111.","abuse","leave_abuser,gbvhelp")
e("children_abuse","Children affected by abuse","child abuse,my child is being abused,children seeing violence,kids witness abuse,my kids are scared,child safety,abuse of a child,my daughter was touched,child sexual abuse",
"Children are harmed by seeing violence too, and child abuse must always be taken seriously.\n\n• Childline South Africa (free): 0800 055 555\n• Police 10111, or 112 in an emergency\n• Anyone who suspects abuse can report it to the police or a social worker\n• Believe the child, stay calm, don't blame them, and get medical care if needed\n• A protection order can name your children too\n\nYou are not overreacting by asking for help.","abuse","protection_order,leave_abuser,afterassault")
e("friend_abused","Helping a friend who is being abused","friend is being abused,my friend is abused,help a friend,sister is being abused,support a victim,how do i help someone,my friend gets hit,friend in abusive",
"You can make a big difference.\n• Listen without judging. Say 'I believe you. It's not your fault.'\n• Don't tell her to just leave. Leaving is complicated and risky.\n• Offer practical help: a safe place to talk, a lift, storing documents\n• Share the GBV Command Centre number: 0800 428 428\n• Respect her choices, and keep checking in\n• If she is in immediate danger, call 112 or 10111\n\nLook after yourself too. Supporting someone is hard.","abuse","gbvhelp,safety_plan")
e("afterassault","After a sexual assault: health steps","pep,post exposure,morning after,after rape,emergency contraception after rape,hiv after assault,thuthuzela,after assault,sexual assault care",
"Medical steps that protect your health:\n• HIV PEP (post-exposure prophylaxis): start within 72 hours, the sooner the better\n• Emergency contraception: as early as possible, up to 5 days depending on the type\n• Testing and treatment for STIs, and a pregnancy test\n• Care for injuries and emotional support\n\nThuthuzela Care Centres at many hospitals offer all of this in one place. If you can, don't wash before an exam, but you can still get care if you have.\n\nGBV Command Centre: 0800 428 428 (free, 24/7).","info","rape,reporting,trauma")
e("reporting","Reporting a crime to the police","report to police,open a case,how to report,case number,lay a charge,report rape,report assault,police wont help,police station,sapS",
"You can report at any police station, and you have rights:\n• Ask for a female officer and a private room\n• Have a support person with you\n• You must get a case number and a copy of your statement\n• Police must help you get medical care and a protection order\n• If police refuse or are rude, ask for the station commander, or complain to IPID or the Commission for Gender Equality\n• You choose if and when to report. Sexual offence cases can be reported even years later\n\nCrime Stop (anonymous tip-offs): 08600 10111.","info","protection_order,afterassault,legalaid")
e("legalaid","Free legal help","legal aid,free lawyer,need a lawyer,cant afford a lawyer,legal advice,legal help,my rights,know my rights",
"Free legal help in South Africa:\n• Legal Aid South Africa: 0800 110 110\n• Magistrate's court clerks can help with protection orders and maintenance applications\n• Law clinics at universities\n• Commission for Gender Equality and the Public Protector for complaints\n\nHerHub's Know your rights guide in the Information Hub explains protection orders, harassment and reporting. This is general information, not legal advice.","info","protection_order,maintenance,reporting")
e("maintenance","Child maintenance","maintenance,child support,alimony,father wont pay,baby daddy,maintenance court,support for my child,he doesn't pay for the child,child grant",
"Both parents must support their children, whether or not they were married.\n\n• Apply at the maintenance court at your magistrate's court. It's free.\n• Bring the child's birth certificate, your ID, proof of expenses and the other parent's details\n• A maintenance officer helps you, and the court can order payments, even deducted from salary\n• Not paying can lead to penalties\n• Ask SASSA about child support grants too\n\nLegal Aid: 0800 110 110.","finance","economic_abuse,legalaid")
e("marital_rape","Rape within a relationship","marital rape,my husband forces me,rape in marriage,partner forced sex,boyfriend forced me,no means no in a relationship,coerced into sex,pressured into sex,cant say no to my husband",
"Sex without consent is rape, including in marriage or a relationship. In South Africa the law protects you.\n\n• You can say no at any time, even if you said yes before\n• Being pressured, threatened or too scared to say no is not consent\n• You can report to the police, get medical care, and apply for a protection order\n• GBV Command Centre: 0800 428 428\n\nIt is not your fault.","abuse","rape,protection_order,afterassault",1)
e("consent","What is consent?","what is consent,consent,did i consent,is it rape if,drunk sex consent,said yes but,changed my mind,sober consent",
"Consent means a clear, free and enthusiastic yes, every time.\n• It can be withdrawn at any moment\n• Silence, being asleep, drunk or drugged, or scared is not consent\n• Saying yes to one thing is not yes to everything\n• Pressure, threats or manipulation cancel consent\n\nIf something happened that felt wrong, your feelings are valid and you can talk to a counsellor: GBV Command Centre 0800 428 428.","info","rape,afterassault")
e("sextortion","Sextortion and intimate image abuse","sextortion,blackmail,nudes,leaked nudes,revenge porn,intimate images,someone has my photos,threatening to share my photos,fake nudes,deepfake,naked photo,pay or he will share",
"Threatening to share intimate images is a crime. Under the Cybercrimes Act, sharing them without consent is also illegal.\n\n• Don't pay. Payments usually lead to more demands.\n• Stop replying, but save screenshots, usernames and links as evidence\n• Report the account to the platform and ask them to remove the images\n• Report to the police and ask for a case number\n• You can apply for a protection order\n• Tell someone you trust. This is not your fault.\n\nSupport: GBV Command Centre 0800 428 428.","abuse","cyberstalk,reporting,harassment_order",1)
e("stalking","Stalking","stalk,stalked,stalker,someone is watching me,following me online,showing up at my work,leaves gifts,keeps calling me,obsessed with me",
"Stalking is repeated, unwanted attention that makes you afraid. You can act on it:\n• Keep a log with dates, times, screenshots and photos\n• Tell trusted people, work and security\n• Vary your routines and avoid sharing live locations\n• Report to the police and request a case number\n• Apply for a protection order under the Protection from Harassment Act\n• Use HerHub's Trusted Circle and Safe Journey when going out\n\nIf you are in danger now, call 112 or 10111.","abuse","harassment_order,cyberstalk,reporting")
e("cyberstalk","Phone or online monitoring","spyware,stalkerware,phone is hacked,someone reads my messages,tracking my phone,location tracking,my ex tracks me,check if my phone is monitored,hacked my account,whatsapp web,find my phone tracking",
"Signs your phone may be monitored: fast battery drain, unknown apps, unusual data use, or someone knowing things you never told them.\n\n• Use a safe device (a friend's phone) to change passwords\n• Review installed apps and permissions; remove anything you don't recognise\n• Check 'linked devices' on WhatsApp and your social accounts\n• Turn off location sharing, and check Find My Device or Google account access\n• If it is serious, a factory reset can help. Back up first\n• Keep evidence and report to the police\n\nSee HerHub's Digital & Personal Security checklist.","security","passwords,sextortion,stalking")
e("workplace","Workplace sexual harassment","workplace harassment,harassed at work,boss harasses me,sexual harassment at work,my manager touches,colleague harasses,inappropriate comments at work,hr complaint,ccma",
"Sexual harassment at work is unlawful in South Africa, and your employer must act.\n• Write down what happened: dates, times, witnesses\n• Keep messages and emails\n• Report in writing to your employer or HR and ask for the policy and process\n• You can refer a dispute to the CCMA, or speak to a labour lawyer or Legal Aid (0800 110 110)\n• You can also report to the police if it involves assault\n• You may not be punished for reporting\n\nYou don't have to carry this alone.","info","legalaid,reporting")
e("streetharass","Street harassment","street harassment,catcalling,cat calling,men shout at me,unwanted attention in public,groped on taxi,groped in public,flashing,someone exposed himself",
"Street harassment is not your fault and you don't owe anyone a response.\n• Move to a busy place and stay near other women or staff\n• You can say a loud, firm 'Stop' to draw attention\n• Note details: time, place, description, and report to the police or the transport operator\n• If you are groped or exposed to, you can lay a charge\n• Tell a friend and share your location\n\nIf you feel unsafe now, call 10111 or 112.","sos","reporting,walkhome,taxi")
e("trafficking","Human trafficking and fake job offers","trafficking,human trafficking,fake job offer abroad,forced labour,someone took my passport,job overseas too good,modelling scam,forced prostitution,sold,was lured",
"Warning signs: a job offer that is too good to be true, someone keeping your ID or passport, owing 'debt' you can't pay, being kept from leaving or contacting family.\n\n• Human Trafficking Helpline (free): 0800 222 777\n• Police: 10111, or 112\n• Never travel for work unless you can verify the employer and keep your own documents\n• Tell a trusted person your destination and share your location\n\nYou are not in trouble for being a victim.","abuse","reporting,onlinedating")
e("forcedmarriage","Forced marriage or virginity testing","forced marriage,ukuthwala,arranged marriage against my will,forced to marry,child marriage,virginity test,fgm",
"No one may force you to marry, and a person under 18 cannot lawfully be married off. Forced marriage and ukuthwala are criminal.\n\n• Call Childline (0800 055 555) if you are under 18\n• Call the GBV Command Centre (0800 428 428) or the police (10111)\n• The Commission for Gender Equality can also help\n• You can get a protection order and be placed in a safe place\n\nYou have the right to say no.","abuse","shelter,reporting")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- Personal safety
e("walkhome","Walking home safely","walking home,walk alone,walk at night,safe to walk,walking alone,late night walk,jogging alone,running alone,dark street,safe route home",
"For walking safely:\n• Share your route and expected arrival time with someone. HerHub's Safe Journey does this with a check-in timer\n• Stick to well-lit, busy streets, even if longer\n• Keep your phone charged and one ear free of headphones\n• Walk with purpose and look confident. Trust your gut if something feels off\n• Have keys ready before you reach the door\n• If you are being followed, cross the road, enter a shop, and call someone\n\nWant me to open Safe Journey?","journey","selfdefence,danger,taxi,drivesafe")
e("taxi","Taxi, minibus and ride-hailing safety","uber,bolt,taxi,minibus,ride hailing,e-hailing,lift,cab,inDrive,taxi rank,public transport,train safety,bus safety",
"For rides:\n• Match the driver's name, plate and car to the app before getting in\n• Share your trip with a trusted contact, and sit behind the front passenger seat if alone\n• Keep your phone charged, and don't let the driver change the route without checking\n• Wait inside a shop or building until the car arrives\n• Avoid empty carriages and stay near the driver or other women on trains and buses\n• If you feel unsafe, ask to be dropped at a busy place or call 112\n\nUse Safe Journey to share your trip.","journey","walkhome,drivesafe")
e("drivesafe","Driving, hijacking and car safety","hijack,hijacking,driving alone,car safety,carjack,smash and grab,stopped by police,fake police,flat tyre at night,parking safety,petrol station,broke down",
"In a car:\n• Lock doors and keep windows up in traffic; keep valuables out of sight\n• Park in well-lit, busy places; check the back seat before you get in\n• If someone tries to hijack you: stay calm, don't resist, hand over the vehicle, and get away safely. Your life matters more\n• If a car flashes you or you are followed, drive to a police station or busy place\n• For a breakdown at night, call someone and stay locked in. Hazards on\n• If police stop you and you are unsure, drive slowly to a well-lit public place and ask for ID\n\nEmergency: 112 or 10111.","sos","walkhome,danger")
e("nightout","Nights out, drink spiking","drink spiking,spiked drink,night out,nightclub,party safety,drugged,roofied,drink laced,watch my drink,getting drunk safely,clubbing,going out with friends",
"Staying safe on a night out:\n• Go with friends and agree to leave together. Check in with each other\n• Watch your drink being poured, keep it in hand, and don't accept drinks from strangers\n• Signs of spiking: sudden dizziness, confusion, blurred vision, nausea, memory gaps. Tell a friend or staff, and get to a safe place\n• If you suspect spiking, go to a hospital as soon as you can. Tests are time-sensitive\n• Have your own way home and charged phone\n• Share your location with a trusted contact\n\nIf you were assaulted: 0800 428 428.","journey","rape,afterassault,onlinedating")
e("onlinedating","Online dating and meeting strangers","online dating,dating safely,first date,meeting someone online,tinder,bumble,catfish,romance scam,meet a stranger,blind date,tinder swindler",
"Safer dating:\n• Video call before meeting; reverse-search their photos\n• Meet in a public place and arrange your own transport\n• Tell a friend where you are and who you're with. Use Safe Journey\n• Don't share your home address, workplace or financial details early on\n• Watch your drink and keep your phone charged\n• Red flags: refuses video calls, pushes quickly for love, asks for money or intimate photos\n• Trust your gut. You can leave at any time\n\nIf someone asked for money, see my scam tips.","journey","scam,sextortion,romance_scam")
e("homesafety","Home safety and living alone","home safety,living alone,safe at home,burglar,break in,lock my door,someone tried to enter,security at home,strange man at door,intruder",
"At home:\n• Keep doors and windows locked, even when home. Good lighting outside\n• Don't open the door to unknown people. Ask for ID through a window or intercom\n• Don't announce on social media that you are alone or away\n• Keep emergency numbers visible and a charged phone by the bed\n• Know your neighbours, and join a community safety group\n• If someone is inside, get out or lock yourself in a room, call 10111 or 112\n\nHerHub's SOS can alert your Trusted Circle silently.","sos","danger,circle")
e("loadshedding","Safety during load-shedding","load shedding,loadshedding,power cut,blackout,no electricity safety,dark outside,electricity off",
"During power cuts:\n• Have torches, a charged power bank and your phone's flashlight ready\n• Avoid walking in the dark. Plan trips around the schedule\n• Keep gates and doors locked; traffic lights may be off, so drive carefully\n• Let someone know where you are and when you expect to be home\n• Check on neighbours who live alone\n\nHerHub's siren and flashlight blink are available on the SOS screen.","sos","homesafety,walkhome")
e("travel","Travelling safely","travel safety,travelling alone,solo travel,holiday safety,going abroad,hotel safety,hitchhiking,airbnb safety,long distance",
"Before you travel:\n• Share your itinerary and accommodation with a trusted contact\n• Keep copies of your ID and tickets, and save emergency numbers for where you're going\n• Choose well-reviewed accommodation, and check locks on arrival\n• Keep valuables hidden, avoid showing wealth, and don't hitchhike\n• Set check-in times with a friend\n• Outside South Africa, learn the local emergency number\n\nHerHub's Safe Journey can be used for road trips too.","journey","taxi,walkhome")
e("selfdefence","Self-defence and pepper spray","self defence,self-defence,pepper spray,how to defend myself,fight back,self defense class,taser,alarm keyring,weapon,krav maga",
"Your best defence is avoiding danger, but these help:\n• Use your voice: shout 'Back off!' or 'Fire!' to draw attention\n• Target vulnerable areas (eyes, nose, throat, groin, knees) and then run to safety\n• Pepper spray is legal to carry for personal protection in South Africa, but must be used responsibly. Keep it easy to reach and practise\n• A personal alarm or HerHub's siren draws attention\n• A short self-defence class builds confidence\n\nYou are never to blame for not fighting back. Survival comes first.","sos","danger,sirenhelp")
e("sirenhelp","Using the siren and flashlight","siren,panic alarm,alarm sound,flashlight,torch,loud alarm,make noise,panic button,siren not working,alarm volume",
"HerHub's panic siren is a real, loud emergency siren at full volume. The flashlight blinks 10 times when it starts to draw attention.\n\n• Open Personal Safety & Emergency and tap 'Sound siren', or use the Panic siren shortcut on Home\n• Tap again to stop\n• Turn on 'Also sound the siren' under Hands-free SOS to play it automatically\n• Make sure silent or do-not-disturb mode isn't muting alarms on your phone\n\nWant me to open it?","sos","danger,handsfree")
e("campus","Campus and school safety","campus safety,university safety,res safety,student safety,lecturer harass,school safety,sexual harassment at university,campus security,student housing",
"On campus:\n• Save campus security's number and share your route when walking after dark\n• Use well-lit paths and walk with friends\n• Report harassment or assault to campus protection services, the university's gender-based violence office, and the police\n• Universities must have policies against sexual misconduct. Ask for them\n• Look after friends at parties and agree to leave together\n\nSupport: GBV Command Centre 0800 428 428.","info","reporting,nightout,workplace")
e("atm","ATM and money safety","atm safety,atm,cash withdrawal,someone watched my pin,card swallowed,card skimming,safe banking,cash in public",
"At the ATM:\n• Use machines inside banks or well-lit, busy places\n• Cover the keypad when entering your PIN. Never accept 'help' from strangers\n• If the card is stuck or swallowed, call your bank from the number on the card, don't leave it\n• Don't count cash in public, and stay aware of who is behind you\n• Use your banking app instead when possible\n\nNever share your PIN or OTP with anyone.","finance","scam,otp,simswap")

# ---------------------------------------------------------------- helplines
e("gbvhelp","GBV and abuse helplines","gbv,gender based violence,gender-based violence,helpline,hotline,gbv number,gbv command centre,stop gender violence,tears foundation,who can i call,numbers for abuse,counselling for abuse",
"Free help for gender-based violence in South Africa:\n• GBV Command Centre (24/7, free): 0800 428 428\n• Stop Gender Violence Helpline: 0800 150 150\n• TEARS Foundation USSD: *134*7355# (free 'Please call me')\n• Childline: 0800 055 555\n• Human Trafficking Helpline: 0800 222 777\n• Police: 10111, Emergency: 112\n\nTap a number to call. You can see more in the Emergency Resource Directory.","directory","abuse_now,shelter,legalaid")
e("mentalhelp","Mental health helplines","mental health line,counselling number,sadag,lifeline,therapy number,who to call for depression,mental health helpline,talk to someone,speak to a counsellor",
"Support lines in South Africa:\n• SADAG: 0800 21 22 23\n• SADAG Suicide Crisis Line: 0800 567 567\n• SADAG 24-hour helpline: 0800 12 13 14\n• Lifeline: 0861 322 322\n• Emergency: 112\n\nYou can also visit a public clinic for referral to a counsellor. You deserve support.","mind","suicide,depression,therapy")
e("emergency","Emergency numbers","emergency number,number for police,ambulance number,10111,10177,112,fire brigade,paramedics,emergency services,er24,netcare 911",
"Key emergency numbers (South Africa):\n• 112 from any mobile phone\n• Police: 10111\n• Public ambulance: 10177\n• Crime Stop: 08600 10111\n• GBV Command Centre: 0800 428 428\n\nIn an emergency, HerHub's SOS screen has call buttons and can alert your Trusted Circle.","sos","gbvhelp,mentalhelp")
